{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>I stood between HaShem and between you at that time, to declare to you the word of HaShem</b> — for he would go up the mountain and hear the words of HaShem, and come down and tell them, until HaShem spoke with them face to face. And this is a reference to all that is mentioned in the section 'In the third month' from the beginning of 'Thus you shall say to the house of Jacob' until 'and HaShem said to him, Go down' (Exodus 19:3–24). And R. Abraham [Ibn Ezra] said (Ibn Ezra on Deuteronomy 5:5) that this was after the standing at Mount Sinai, and the meaning is: 'He spoke with you face to face, and from that day I was standing between HaShem and between you.' This is not correct. And the correct view in my eyes is that it alludes to what we explained there (Exodus 20:7): that Israel did not hear from the mouth of the Gevurah anything but 'I am' and 'You shall have no other god,' and the rest of the commandments — Moses is the one who declared to them whatever HaShem would say. And this is 'to declare to you the word of HaShem' — to explain to you each and every utterance. The meaning of <b>between HaShem and between you</b> is that at the time of the commandments he was closer to the place of the Shekhinah than they were, for they had kept their distance, as I explained there (Exodus 20:15), and this is 'for you were afraid.' <b>And you did not come up the mountain</b> — you did not draw near to Him though you had the permission to do so; therefore I was standing between you and the place of Glory in the middle, for the distance between you and Him was great, and from there I would declare the commandments to you." "<b>Saying</b> — this refers back to 'HaShem spoke with you on the mountain from the midst of the fire, saying' — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 5:5). And R. Abraham [Ibn Ezra] said (Ibn Ezra on Deuteronomy 5:5): Do not be troubled by the fact that the word לֵאמֹר is far removed, for so are many such instances, and the Speaker did not divide verses or sections. And it is more fitting to attach it to 'to declare to you the word of HaShem, saying,' in the manner of 'and you shall tell your son on that day, saying' (Exodus 13:8). But in my opinion there is no need for this at all; rather, its meaning is that 'for you were afraid of the fire and you did not come up the mountain, saying: We shall not go up, for we are afraid of the fire' — he recalls that they told him this explicitly, and he does not say so from his own conjecture."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:45:37.739278578Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}